In order to define a generic class we use this symbol <> this will declare a generic class.
For example:
public class Node<T> {
    private T elem;
    public Node(T elem) {
        this.elem = elem;
    }
    public T data() {
        return this.elem;
    }
    ...
}
the T is the Object we will work with and it will work with any class so now when we will make a new object of that
class type we will do Node<String> n = new Node<>("hello"); (you dont have to respecify the string)

...

String s = n.data();

before generics you could make a general class but you could insert into one class multiple different instances
like 1 of the sub objects would be Integer, the next will be String, this can cause confusion.

In generic class you declare the type, then you can only add of that type! other wise you will get compilation error.

